# Copy to .env and set the path for this machine.
#
# Compose reads .env automatically when run from this directory. Without it,
# ${OPENCODE_GLOBAL_CONFIG} is empty and the server/discover/eval volume
# specs collapse to ":/home/harness/.config/opencode/opencode.json:ro",
# which compose rejects with "empty section between colons".
#
# The Makefile defaults this with ?= so shell invocations work regardless.
# Anything driving compose directly -- an orchestrator, a CI runner, a bare
# `docker compose` call -- gets no such default and needs this file.
#
# Must be an absolute path to your global opencode config, which is
# mounted read-only as the harness's provider and auth source.
OPENCODE_GLOBAL_CONFIG=/home/YOUR_USER/.config/opencode/opencode.json

# The uid:gid the containers run as. Everything written to ./results and
# ./notebooks lands with this ownership on the host; without it the
# containers run as root and leave files you cannot edit or delete
# without sudo. `id -u` and `id -g` give the right values.
#
# The Makefile derives both itself, so `make server-up` and friends need
# nothing here. Anything calling compose directly does, as with
# OPENCODE_GLOBAL_CONFIG above -- absent, the compose file falls back to
# 1000:1000, which is right on a single-user machine and wrong anywhere
# else.
HOST_UID=1000
HOST_GID=1000

# This directory's absolute path on the host, used as the prefix for
# every bind-mount source in docker-compose.yml.
#
# Leave it unset for shell use: the compose file defaults it to `.`, and
# compose resolves that against the project directory, which is right
# whenever you are running in this directory.
#
# It is REQUIRED when something else drives compose from a different
# filesystem view -- cicd_runner's coordinator being the case that
# proved it. There, `.` resolved to a path that exists only inside that
# container, the daemon created an empty directory tree at the host root
# rather than erroring, and the server crash-looped on missing
# credentials.
#
# Get the value from the Makefile, which derives it from its own
# location rather than from wherever you happen to be standing:
#
#     make print-harness-root
#
# Not `pwd`, which is only correct if your shell is already in this
# directory, and not `realpath` or `readlink -f`. The same paths are
# resolved by the auth extraction scripts and by Terraform's abspath(),
# neither of which resolves symlinks -- a value that disagrees with them
# describes the same file by a different string, which Terraform reads
# as drift and acts on by recreating the container.
# HARNESS_ROOT=/home/YOUR_USER/path/to/opencode-model-eval

# PROVIDER-REQUEST CAPTURE. Off unless set: this routes every provider
# request through a proxy container, and a bug there breaks every eval
# at once rather than one check.
#
# Set it to record what opencode SENDS the model -- the
# resolved system prompt and tool definitions, which opencode assembles
# per request and never persists, so they exist nowhere else. The
# capture service is already in docker-compose.yml and idle until this
# points at it. Records to results/capture/provider-requests.jsonl;
# credentials are redacted before anything reaches disk.
#
# Scope worth knowing before you rely on it: a plain-HTTP provider (the
# local Ollama path) is fully visible. An HTTPS provider reaches the
# proxy as CONNECT and its body stays encrypted, so a cloud run records
# that a request happened and not what was in it. Each record says which
# of the two it is.
#
# Restart the server after changing this.
# OPENCODE_CAPTURE_PROXY=http://capture:8888

# Hosts the server must NOT route through the capture proxy. The default
# covers loopback and the compose service name, which matters because
# the setting is not scoped to opencode: every client in that container
# reads it, including the session reaper polling localhost and
# opencode's own calls to itself on 0.0.0.0. host.docker.internal is
# excluded on purpose -- provider traffic is the point of capturing.
# OPENCODE_CAPTURE_NO_PROXY=localhost,127.0.0.1,0.0.0.0,::1,server
